# Surfaces

This Access application serves two user-facing surfaces:

- `app` — the Auth and Visa end-user surface
- `org` — the Auth and Visa staff / organization surface

The local `com` user-facing surface is retired by
[`access-surface-hard-reset`](../../../../specs/access-surface-hard-reset/spec.md). Legacy `Com`
models and database state remain subject to the separate model and database review. Cross-repository
OIDC client registrations, including external corporate redirect URIs, do not create local `com`
routes or controllers.

Tech and Palm remain independent relying-party boundaries for their external clients. Tech uses
operator identity and requires its own `tech.access` capability. Palm uses client identity. Neither
boundary inherits another surface's credentials, permissions, or sessions.

Each surface is an independent boundary.

## Do not mix surfaces

Do not share controllers, routes, views, policies, sessions, or state across surfaces unless the
existing code has an explicit, reviewed shared abstraction. One surface must not access another
surface directly.

## Per-surface host constraints

- Each surface must enforce its own host constraints.
- Never assume a default host.
- Cross-surface routing is forbidden.

## Boundary discipline

- Respect the boundary in every layer: request handling, authorization, and persistence.
- Do not store request data so that it can leak across surfaces (see `absolute-rules` on shared
  state).
